That's to be expected. In vanilla MS-DOS, a file name may
have zero or one periods, but not two, which is why the
first periods were changed to underscores for MS-DOS
compilations.

The rest of the story is that both of those files are
_generated_, not original files; the original files probably
had names ending in ".L" or ".Y" (but lowercase) to indicate
that they were respectively in the source language for the
lex() lexical analyzer or the yacc() "yet another compiler
compiler" tools. Running lex() and yacc() (or the GNU
equivalents flex() and bison()) reads *.l and *.y files and
writes C source code and header code files for further
compilation.

This is done in turn because the C code for lexing and
compiler-compiling is extremely repetitive and tedious to
write, but fairly easy to generate from a language better
designed to capture the concepts involved in the two tasks.

